  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThis afternoon water vapor imagery and 500mb model height analysis \nshowed the blocking ridge continued to eroded as shortwaves moved \nacross the northern periphery of the remaining ridge. Downstream an \nupper level low associated with the nor'easter was nearly stationary \nover New England. Troughing was expanding over the western half of \nCanada with the southern extent of the trough splitting over the \nPacific Northwest. At the surface high pressure continued to bring \npleasant fall weather to the U.P. with temperatures warming into the \n60s and 70s along with light winds. Clouds were spreading over the \nwestern half of the U.P. but struggling in their eastward march as \nthey encounter a dry, stable airmass. \n\nUpper level ridging will continue to break down through tomorrow \nwith troughing expected for the midweek. The surface high will hold \non until tomorrow before shifting southeast away from the region. \nThis will result another day of light winds and continued warm \ntemperatures (10-15F above average). With the dry air expect cool \nnight tonight and Monday night along with the potential for fog with \nstrong radiational cooling. For tonight fog is likely to form over \nthe western half of the U.P. (some cloud cover should limit cooling \nin the central/east) for the early morning hours Monday. The fog \ncould be dense and folks may want to leave themselves additional \ntime for their morning commute. Fog will be possible over the entire \nU.P. Monday night, especially over the interior locations.\n\nFor Tuesday into Wednesday west-southwest flow will overspread the \nregion as troughing moves into central Canada and the northern \nplains. Meanwhile, the trough over the Pacific Northwest will cut \noff over the four-corners region and track into the southern Plains. \nThis will bring increasing Pacific and Gulf moisture transport \nnortheast into the U.P. with PWATs reaching up to 1.25\" (90th \npercentile from climo) Tuesday night through Wednesday. With \ndewpoints pushing 60F on Wednesday it will feel a bit humid for \nfall. There will be multiple opportunities for precipitation Tuesday \nnight through Wednesday night as warm, moist air advection increases \nalong with a 40-45kt low level jet providing a focus for large scale \nascent. Then a cold front will push through the area by Thursday \nmorning with additional showers preceding it and cooler, drier air \nbehind it. As far as rainfall amounts, NBM probabilities are around \n20-50% for 0.5\"+ of rainfall (highest in the east) and 50-60% for \n0.25\"+ across the entire U.P. With deep moisture would expect that \nsome locations will overachieve with localized heavy downpours. \n\nExpect cooler, more seasonable temperatures to finish out the week \nand into next weekend with highs in the low 60s. Wouldn't rule out \nlake effect rain showers, especially over the eastern U.P. on \nThursday night into Friday morning as a passing shortwave brings the \npotential for deeper inversion heights and colder 850mb temps. The \nGEFS is the most aggressive among the ensembles with a mean of 0C at \n850mb while the LREF (EC/GEFS/CMC) mean sits around 3C with 0C \nfalling into the 10th percentile. With lake temperatures sitting \naround 13-16C would expect a high potential for lake effect \ncloudiness and a few light showers even with the warmer, mean \nsolution.",
